# PWKM Scripts Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMPORTANT: what .env does and does not cover
#
# .env supplies CREDENTIALS ONLY. The scripts that need Notion or Google
# credentials load this file explicitly.
#
# The settings variables further down (LOCAL_TIMEZONE and the PWKM_* group)
# are read from the PROCESS ENVIRONMENT, not from this file. Most scripts
# never load .env, and the two that do read their module-level settings before
# the load happens. Putting those variables here will silently have no effect:
# no error, no warning, just the default value.
#
# Set them as real environment variables instead. For example, on Windows:
#     setx LOCAL_TIMEZONE "Europe/London"
# or on macOS and Linux, in your shell profile:
#     export LOCAL_TIMEZONE="Europe/London"
#
# They are documented below so you know they exist and what they do.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === Credentials: set these HERE, in .env ===

# Notion API key.
# Create an integration at https://www.notion.so/my-integrations and copy the
# "Internal Integration Secret". Grant the integration access to every
# database the scripts touch, not just the Tasks database.
NOTION_API_KEY=your_notion_api_key_here

# Notion database ID for your Tasks database.
# Found in the database URL: https://notion.so/DATABASE_ID?v=...
# The 32-character string, with or without hyphens.
NOTION_DATABASE_ID=your_database_id_here

# Google Calendar OAuth credentials.
# Create a project at https://console.cloud.google.com/, enable the Google
# Calendar API, then create OAuth 2.0 credentials of type "Desktop app".
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret_here


# === Settings: set these in the PROCESS ENVIRONMENT, not here ===

# LOCAL_TIMEZONE
#   An IANA timezone name, for example America/New_York or Europe/London.
#   Used by every script that formats or compares times. If unset, scripts
#   fall back to a North American Eastern default, which is almost certainly
#   wrong for you.

# PWKM_PYTHON
#   Path to the Python interpreter the startup script should use when it
#   invokes helper scripts. Defaults to whichever interpreter is running it.
#   Set this if that is not the one you want.

# PWKM_STATE_DIR
#   Directory holding persistent state files (session timer, audit state) and
#   the running-summaries subdirectory. Defaults to the parent of the scripts
#   directory.

# PWKM_TASKS_CSV
#   Full path to the exported tasks CSV. Only consulted as a fallback: the
#   scripts look beside themselves first, which is where the export lands by
#   default. Set it if you keep the CSV somewhere else.

# PWKM_HEALTH_DB
#   Full path to the health database. Optional. Leaving it unset disables the
#   health section of the startup report rather than causing an error.

# PWKM_SECONDARY_CALENDARS
#   Comma-separated calendar IDs to merge into the calendar view alongside
#   your primary calendar. Useful for a work calendar that exposes only
#   free/busy. Leave unset for primary-calendar-only.